Lab 15: Setting Up Floating IPs for Public Access in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Allocate floating IPs from an OpenStack public pool.
Associate floating IPs with instances to enable public access.
Configure security groups and firewalls to ensure secure public connectivity.
Prerequisites
Before starting, ensure you have:

Basic Linux command-line knowledge.
An active OpenStack environment (provided by Al Nafi—click Start Lab to access).
A running OpenStack instance (VM) to associate with the floating IP.
Admin or member access to an OpenStack project.
Lab Setup
Note: Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux cloud machines. Click Start Lab to begin—no additional setup is required.

Task 1: Allocate Floating IPs
Step 1.1: Log in to OpenStack Dashboard
Open a terminal on your Linux machine.
Authenticate with OpenStack using the CLI:
source openrc.sh  # Provided by Al Nafi; contains credentials
Expected Output: No errors; you’re now authenticated.
Step 1.2: List Available Floating IP Pools
openstack network list --external
Expected Output:

+--------------------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+
| ID                                   | Name       | Subnets                              |
+--------------------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+
| 1234abcd-5678-90ef-ghij-klmnopqrstuv | public     | 5678abcd-90gh-ijkl-mnop-qrstuvwxyz12 |
+--------------------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+
Step 1.3: Allocate a Floating IP
openstack floating ip create public
Expected Output:

+---------------------+--------------------------------------+
| Field               | Value                                |
+---------------------+--------------------------------------+
| floating_ip_address | 203.0.113.10                         |
| id                  | abcd1234-5678-90ef-ghij-klmnopqrstuv |
+---------------------+--------------------------------------+
Troubleshooting: If no IPs are available, contact your OpenStack admin.

Task 2: Associate Floating IPs with Instances
Step 2.1: List Running Instances
openstack server list
Expected Output:

+--------------------------------------+------------+--------+---------------------+
| ID                                   | Name       | Status | Networks            |
+--------------------------------------+------------+--------+---------------------+
| 5678abcd-90gh-ijkl-mnop-qrstuvwxyz12 | my-instance| ACTIVE | private=192.0.2.5   |
+--------------------------------------+------------+--------+---------------------+
Step 2.2: Associate the Floating IP
Replace <FLOATING_IP> and <INSTANCE_ID> with your values:

openstack server add floating ip <INSTANCE_ID> <FLOATING_IP>
Example:

openstack server add floating ip 5678abcd-90gh-ijkl-mnop-qrstuvwxyz12 203.0.113.10
Verification:

openstack server show my-instance | grep addresses
Expected Output:

| addresses | private=192.0.2.5, 203.0.113.10 |
Task 3: Configure Security for Public Access
Step 3.1: Allow SSH/HTTP Traffic
Find your instance’s security group:
openstack server show my-instance -c security_groups -f value
Add rules to allow inbound traffic:
openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 22 <SECURITY_GROUP_NAME>
openstack security group rule create --proto tcp --dst-port 80 <SECURITY_GROUP_NAME>
Expected Output: Rules are added without errors.
Step 3.2: Test Public Access
ping 203.0.113.10  # Replace with your floating IP
Expected Outcome: Successful ping replies.

Conclusion
You’ve successfully:

Allocated a floating IP from OpenStack’s public pool.
Associated it with an instance to enable public access.
Configured security groups to allow safe SSH/HTTP traffic.
Why It Matters: Floating IPs are critical for exposing cloud services to the internet while maintaining flexibility and security.

Troubleshooting Tips
No Floating IPs Available: Contact your OpenStack administrator.
SSH Connection Fails: Verify security group rules and instance firewall settings.
Command Errors: Ensure you’re authenticated (source openrc.sh).
